can you try to understand my directory what i wa s try to archive and this is my ticket ''Purpose

Investigate and document the ew1r-rep-01 monitoring and reporting environment (ew1r-rep-01.ad.shnonprd.kurtosys-internal.net) to establish a complete inventory of workloads, consumers, and dependencies, and produce a decommission readiness assessment with retire / replace / move recommendations.

This epic delivers read-only discovery and documentation only. It does not decommission the server, disable SQL Agent jobs, change Grafana configs, migrate dashboards, or alter production monitoring (separate follow-on epic(s)).

Background — the problem

The host started as a SQL Server monitoring box but now also runs Grafana, SQL Agent jobs, and monitoring for other systems (RDS MySQL, SingleStore, and others). Platform Engineering intends to decommission this server, but there is no authoritative inventory of what it runs, what it monitors, who consumes the output, or what would break if it were switched off.

Without a structured discovery pass, decommission risks silent loss of alerts, orphaned Grafana dashboards, and undocumented SQL Agent jobs that teams still rely on.

Why this epic exists

Decommission safety — know every job, dashboard, alert, and external target before any change.

Operational clarity — document who uses this server and how (URL/DNS, teams, alert recipients).

Migration planning — classify each component as retire, replace (e.g. CloudWatch, Grafana Cloud, unified monitoring), or move to another host.

Alignment with unified database monitoring initiatives (TECH-3428, TECH-3409) where overlap exists.

Objectives

Gain read-only access and explore the server without changing configuration or disabling jobs.

Document SQL Server: databases, SQL Agent jobs, linked servers, and alert mechanisms.

Document Grafana: datasources, dashboards, alert rules, access URL/DNS, and folder structure.

Document external monitoring targets (RDS MySQL, SingleStore, other instances) and connection method.

Identify consumers (teams/people) and infrastructure dependencies (DNS, firewall, service accounts, runbooks).

Produce a topology diagram or equivalent bullet flow: server → targets monitored → consumers.

Deliver retire / replace / move classification and scoped backlog for decommission follow-on epic(s).

Scope

In scope

Read-only login and exploration of ew1r-rep-01.

SQL Agent job inventory table (name, schedule, purpose, alert target, or "unknown").

Grafana datasource and dashboard inventory (folder names sufficient; flag business-critical items).

External systems monitored and how the host connects to each.

Consumer list (teams/people) or "unknown" where not confirmed.

Dependencies: DNS, firewall rules, service accounts, credential vault references (no secrets in docs).

Open questions list for items needing a second pair of eyes.

Confluence document (or equivalent) linked from this epic.

Manager review and sign-off that documentation is ready for decommission planning.

Out of scope (explicit)

Decommissioning, powering off, or repurposing the server.

Disabling SQL Agent jobs or changing Grafana/datasource configuration.

Migrating dashboards or alerts to a replacement platform (follow-on epic).

Building replacement monitoring (see TECH-3428 unified monitoring where applicable).

Child work items under this epic until breakdown is agreed.

Delivery themes

Theme A — Access and SQL Server inventory

Obtain access; confirm read-only exploration approach with manager.

Catalog databases, linked servers, and SQL Agent jobs with schedules and alert paths.

Theme B — Grafana and alerting

Inventory datasources, dashboards, folders, and alert rules.

Document how users reach Grafana (URL, DNS, auth).

Theme C — External targets and consumers

Map RDS MySQL, SingleStore, and other monitored instances to connection method.

Identify teams or people who use dashboards or receive alerts; escalate unknowns.

Theme D — Classification and handover

Topology diagram or bullet flow: server → targets → consumers.

Per-component recommendation: retire, replace, or move (with rationale).

Scoped backlog for decommission / migration follow-on epic.

Constraints (read-only)

Do not disable jobs, change configs, or delete anything on the server.

Do not put passwords or secrets in the ticket or Confluence document. Reference vault/credential location only.

Escalate when access is blocked, purpose of a job/dashboard is unclear, or usage cannot be confirmed.

Dependencies

Server access (RDP/SSH and SQL/Grafana credentials via approved vault paths).

Input from teams who may consume Grafana dashboards or SQL Agent alerts.

Network/firewall documentation for external target connectivity.

Risks

Access delays — mitigated by early escalation to manager and IT.

Undocumented legacy jobs — mitigated by "unknown" flagging and open questions list rather than guessing.

Scope creep into decommission execution — mitigated by explicit out-of-scope and follow-on epic.

Definition of Done (epic closure)

One Confluence document linked from this epic covering SQL Server, Grafana, external targets, alerting, dependencies, and consumers.

SQL Agent jobs table complete (name, schedule, purpose, alerts — or "unknown").

Grafana datasources and dashboards listed; critical items flagged.

External monitored systems and connection methods documented.

Topology diagram or equivalent flow published.

Open questions list captured and assigned where possible.

Retire / replace / move classification per major component.

Manager review complete; epic marked ready for decommission planning follow-on.

Success criteria (measurable)

100% of SQL Agent jobs on the host captured in the inventory table (or explicitly marked unknown with escalation note).

All Grafana datasources and dashboard folders listed.

Every known external monitoring target documented with connection method.

Manager sign-off that documentation is sufficient to plan decommission without further discovery.

Related work

TECH-3428 — Unified database performance and capacity insights (potential replace target for some monitoring).

TECH-3409 — Aurora PostgreSQL Serverless v2 monitoring review.

Follow-on (not this epic)

Decommission execution, Grafana migration, SQL Agent job replacement, and DNS/firewall cutover will be tracked under separate PROJECT epic(s) once this investigation and documentation are complete.


