Context
Great! Let me walk you through the diagram layer by layer.

Layer 1 — The Server (Top, Blue box)
EW1R-REP-01 at 10.72.8.216

This is the server you investigated. It sits on Windows Server 2019 and it's a non-production host — meaning it was never meant to be this important, but it grew into a critical piece of infrastructure over time. This is exactly why Platform Engineering wants to decommission it properly rather than just switch it off.

Layer 2 — What's Running On It
Two things run on this server:

SQL Server 2019 (yellow) — the engine running 60 jobs, 8 databases, 97 linked servers

Grafana (pink/red) — the dashboard tool that 3 people actively use

Layer 3 — The Databases (the most important layer)
Colour	Meaning
🟢 Green	Active and healthy
🟡 Yellow	Needs investigation
🔴 Red	Critical problem
DBA_VCC_AWS 180GB 🟢 — collecting KAPP API data every 15 minutes, 563 million rows. This is the heart of KAPP observability

DBA_VCC_MEMSQL 77GB 🔴 — jobs were disabled in May 2026, month-end dashboards have been broken ever since. Nobody noticed. Raise this Monday

DBA_VCC_COST 5GB 🟡 — tracks every client's usage every Sunday. It's on FULL recovery meaning someone made it unlosable. Likely feeds client billing — confirm Monday

DBA_VCC_MYSQL 26GB 🟢 — MySQL and RDS monitoring, healthy

DBA_VCC 21GB 🟢 — core framework, Encore and BNY logs

DBA_VCC_ATLASSIAN 2GB 🟡 — Jira data, consumer unknown

KURTOSYS_BASELINE 51GB 🟡 — performance baselines, consumer unknown

Utilities 🟢 — DBA scripts, healthy

Layer 4 — External Targets (What It Collects From)
These are all the systems EW1R-REP-01 reaches out to and pulls data from:

🟢 EW2P-MSSQL-01/02 — production SQL Servers in EU-West-2, actively monitored

🟡 EW1P-OCT RDS — AWS RDS instance, backup job running but ownership unknown

